      "id": 1,
      "text": "Knox began as Edenburg in 1853, then renamed after the late‑19th‑century oil boom spurred hotels, saloons, and stores.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/knox_pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Quaker farmer Mendenhall bought 160 acres in what is now Elk Township, naming the area Eden for its sheltered ridge under Canoe Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/knox_pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Mendenhall opened the Eden Inn, an early inn and entertainment spot that served travelers at this rural crossroads.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/knox_pennsylvania",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Pennsylvania Route 208 cuts through Knox, linking the town eastward to U.S. 322 and westward to Shippenville.",
